    "https://ukjobs.uky.edu/postings/643041": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Criminal Justice",
      "description": "University of Kentucky Applicant Site | Lecturer in Criminal Justice Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Sign Up For Job Alerts! Log In /Create Account Help Applicant Support Lecturer in Criminal Justice Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Posting Details Job Title Lecturer in Criminal Justice Requisition Number FE04971 Working Title Department Name 8T110:College of Social Work Work Location Lexington, KY Type of Position Faculty Position Time Status Full-Time Tenure Track Status Non-Tenure Track Required Education Master of Social Work ( MSW ), Master of Science in Criminal Justice ( MSCJ ), or related field. Required Related Experience Teaching experience at the university level Required License/Registration/Certification n/a Job Summary THE UNIVERSITY OF KENTUCKY , COLLEGE OF SOCIAL WORK (CoSW) is a renowned leader in higher education. Our mission is clear: Through rigorous research, excellence in instruction, and steadfast service, the CoSW works to improve the human condition. Always, in all ways. As the state’s flagship university, CoSW is committed to actualizing our mission through innovative, accessible academic programming. CoSW is home to Bachelor of Arts in Social Work (traditional and online), Bachelor of Arts in Criminal Justice (traditional and online), Bachelor of Science in Criminal Justice (traditional and online), Master of Social Work (hybrid and online), Master of Science in Criminal Justice (online), Doctorate of Social Work (online), PhD in Social Work (traditional), and multiple certificate programs. In addition to Lexington’s main campus, we have a satellite campus at Fort Sam Houston in San Antonio, Texas. To continue to advance our support infrastructure for CoSW, we invite applicants for a 12-month, non- tenure eligible Lecturer position in the Lecturer Title Series to begin as early as Spring or Fall 2027. The University’s mission involves three primary functions across our faculty cohort: instruction, research, and service. Each year, faculty work with the Office of the Dean to delineate their distribution of effort ( DOE ). This DOE outlines how faculty will spend their time. In general, DOE for faculty members in the Lecturer Title Series typically includes allocation in the area of instruction. Of course, the DOE can fluctuate depending on an array of factors (e.g., new course development, service to the college, etc.). While the DOE is reviewed on an ongoing basis, successful candidates are expected to teach undergraduate and graduate courses. It is expected that work in this faculty appointment, including but not limited to course instruction, office hours, administrative meetings, and attending college-wide events such as faculty meetings, will occur on-site. The Lecturer positions will include provision of instruction. Such expectations include conducting classes, seminars, and lectures as part of the courses taught. Additionally, these positions will be utilizing career and working experience to educate students, advise them on both educational and professional levels, and will continuously contribute to their department’s development. While a doctorate in Social Work, Criminal Justice, or a related field is preferred, candidates with an ABD or an appropriate master’s degree and substantial relevant professional experience will be considered. Employment at UK is subject to fund availability and comes with many rewards, focusing on your total well-being and career development, with leadership striving to provide a strong work-life integration. The university generously contributes to employees’ retirement plans, medical coverage, and life insurance. In addition, UK offers optional benefits such as dental and vision insurance, additional retirement plans and much more. To learn more about these benefits, please visit: https://hr.uky.edu/employment/our-benefits . Applicants are requested to submit a curriculum vitae (resume), a letter of interest detailing applicant’s goodness of fit for the position (upload as Cover Letter), contact information for at least three professional references (upload as Specific Request #1), and relevant teaching experience along with evidence of teaching effectiveness for one or more academic years (upload as Specific Request #2). Application reviews will begin right away and continue until the position has been filled. We anticipate this search will run from October through December 2026. Skills / Knowledge / Abilities Does this position have supervisory responsibilities? No Preferred Education/Experience Teaching/instruction in a wide array of courses at multiple levels and formats, including online instruction, is preferred. Deadline to Apply Open Until Filled Yes Our University Community We value the well-being of each of our employees and are dedicated to creating a healthy place for everyone to work, learn and live. In the interest of maintaining a safe and healthy environment for our students, employees, patients and visitors, the University of Kentucky is a Tobacco & Drug Free campus. The University follows both the federal and state Constitutions as well as all applicable federal and state laws on nondiscrimination. The University provides equal opportunities for qualified persons in all aspects of institutional operations and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ethnic origin, religion, creed, age, physical or mental disability, veteran status, uniformed service, political belief, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, marital status, genetic information or social or economic status or whether the person is a smoker or nonsmoker, as long as the person complies with University regulation concerning smoking. Any candidate offered a position may be required to pass pre-employment screenings as mandated by University of Kentucky Human Resources. These screenings may include a national background check and/or drug screen. Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Specific Request 1 Specific Request 2 Optional Documents 805 Las Cimas Parkway Suite 400 Austin, Texas, 78746 © University of Kentucky Lexington, Kentucky 40506 An Equal Opportunity University Accreditation Directory Email Privacy Policy Copyright © Faber To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://ukjobs.uky.edu/postings/643038": {
      "title": "Director of Undergraduate Studies in Criminal Justice - Assistant, Associate, or Professor",
      "description": "University of Kentucky Applicant Site | Director of Undergraduate Studies in Criminal Justice - Assistant, Associate, or Professor Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Sign Up For Job Alerts! Log In /Create Account Help Applicant Support Director of Undergraduate Studies in Criminal Justice - Assistant, Associate, or Professor Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Posting Details Job Title Director of Undergraduate Studies in Criminal Justice - Assistant, Associate, or Professor Requisition Number FE04969 Working Title Department Name 8T110:College of Social Work Work Location Lexington, KY Type of Position Faculty Position Time Status Full-Time Tenure Track Status Tenure Track Required Education PhD in Criminology and/or Criminal Justice Required Related Experience Demonstrable administrative experience is preferred, although applicants with significant engagement with criminal justice education, leadership, curriculum review and development, will be considered. Strong potential for securing external funding and producing high-quality scholarship. Ability to foster, develop, and/or maintain positive professional relationships with local, state, federal, and private criminal justice focused agencies/organizations/associations. Required License/Registration/Certification n/a Job Summary THE UNIVERSITY OF KENTUCKY , COLLEGE OF SOCIAL WORK (CoSW) is a renowned leader in higher education. Our mission is clear: Through rigorous research, excellence in instruction, and steadfast service, the CoSW works to improve the human condition. Always, in all ways. As the state’s flagship university, CoSW is committed to actualizing our mission through innovative, accessible academic programming. CoSW is home to several undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral programs, including: Bachelor of Arts in Criminal Justice (traditional and online), Bachelor of Science in Criminal Justice (traditional and online), Bachelor of Arts in Social Work (traditional and online), Master of Science in Criminal Justice (online), Master of Social Work (hybrid and online), Doctorate of Social Work (online), Ph.D. in Social Work (traditional), and multiple certificate programs. In addition to Lexington’s main campus, we have a satellite campus at Fort Sam Houston in San Antonio, Texas. The Director of Undergraduate Studies in Criminal Justice serves as the academic leader for the Bachelor of Arts and Bachelor of Science in Criminal Justice programs and undergraduate certificate offerings. Working collaboratively with the Chair and Executive Director of Criminal Justice, the Director provides strategic leadership for curriculum, student success, faculty engagement, program quality, and academic innovation across both in-person and online learning environments. Reporting directly to the Chair and Executive Director, the Director collaborates closely with faculty, staff, college leadership, and university partners to advance educational excellence while supporting enrollment growth, workforce readiness, and continuous improvement across the undergraduate criminal justice enterprise. Primary responsibilities and major areas of emphasis of this position include but are not limited to: Leadership Conceptualize and communicate vision, priorities, and goals for undergraduate degrees and certificate programs in Criminal Justice. Represent the interests of undergraduate programs and certificates at relevant, appropriate international, national, state, and local venues. Provide leadership and support to faculty and/or staff assigned to undergraduate programs and certificates. Student Support and Experience Remain informed about emerging challenges and opportunities affecting undergraduate students and collaborate with faculty and staff to address needs and enhance opportunities. Actively engage in development initiatives that strengthen scholarship support for undergraduate Criminal Justice students. Partner with the Director of Recruitment to implement strategies that increase applications and admissions to the undergraduate Criminal Justice programs. Strategic Planning and Program Development Lead the deployment of new programmatic efforts associated with the undergraduate Criminal Justice programs and certificates. Develop strategic plans and frameworks consistent with programmatic goal attainment, and the broader CoSW and University plans. Oversee the delivery of the BACJ and BSCJ curricula and certificate programs. In cooperation with the Chair and Executive Director of Criminal Justice, submit a deployment schedule for relevant academic semesters. Personnel and Resource Procurement and Oversight Build, submit, and track annual budget requests for the undergraduate Criminal Justice programs and certificates for consideration of the Criminal Justice Chair and Executive Director and Offices of the Dean and ADASA . Recruit and support PTIs assigned to the undergraduate Criminal Justice programs and certificates. Participate in personnel reviews and assessments for faculty teaching in the undergraduate Criminal Justice programs. Quality Control and Reporting Lead annual curricula reviews for the undergraduate Criminal Justice programs. Establish and lead quality assurance reviews and process/procedures, with strict adherence to program specific learning outcomes. Provide performance and other reports associated with the BACJ and BSCJ programs and certificates. The Director of Undergraduate Studies in Criminal Justice will serve on the CoSW Academic Leadership Council and the CoSW Leadership Team. As well, the Undergraduate Studies in Criminal Justice Programs Director serves as an ex officio member of the following college committees: Admissions and Retention, Advising and Student Concerns Committee, and the Curriculum Committee. Other assignments as outlined in the DOE (e.g., research, teaching, and service as assigned by the Dean). The University’s mission involves three primary functions across our faculty cohort: instruction, research, and service. Each year, various ranking faculty work with the Office of the Dean to delineate their distribution of effort ( DOE ). This DOE outlines how faculty will spend their time. In general, DOE for faculty members in the Regular Title Series typically includes allocations in the areas of instruction, research, and service. Of course, the DOE can fluctuate depending on an array of factors (e.g., grant funding, new course development, service to the college, etc.). The DOE for the Director of Undergraduate Studies is subject to negotiation but we anticipate roughly 50% administration, 25% teaching, 20% research, and 5% service. While the DOE is reviewed on an ongoing basis, faculty are expected to develop and maintain a dynamic, externally funded research program and strong record of high-impact scholarship (e.g., publications, presentations, etc.). In addition, successful candidates are expected to teach undergraduate and graduate courses. It is expected that work in this faculty appointment, including but not limited to course instruction, office hours, administrative meetings, and attending college-wide events such as faculty meetings, will occur on-site. Minimum Requirements PhD in Criminology or Criminal Justice Strong potential for securing external funding and producing high-quality scholarship Ability to foster, develop, and/or maintain positive professional relationships with local, state, federal, and private criminal justice focused agencies/organizations/associations Employment at UK is subject to fund availability and comes with many rewards, focusing on your total well-being and career development, with leadership striving to provide a strong work-life integration. The university generously contributes to employees’ retirement plans, medical coverage, and life insurance. In addition, UK offers optional benefits such as dental and vision insurance, additional retirement plans and much more. To learn more about these benefits, please visit: https://hr.uky.edu/employment/our-benefits . Procedure for Candidacy Applicants are requested to submit the following: Letter of interest detailing applicant’s goodness of fit for the position, a brief discussion of administrative leadership experience, research agenda, and relevant teaching experience (upload as Cover Letter) Curriculum Vitae (upload as Resume) Administrative statement that summarizes leadership experience, engagement with criminal justice education, curriculum review and development, research interests, and impact on the criminal justice field (upload as Specific Request #1) Contact information for at least three professional references (upload as Specific Request #2) Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until positions are filled. Inquiries about the position can be emailed to UKCOSW@uky.edu. Skills / Knowledge / Abilities Does this position have supervisory responsibilities? Yes Preferred Education/Experience Deadline to Apply Open Until Filled Yes Our University Community We value the well-being of each of our employees and are dedicated to creating a healthy place for everyone to work, learn and live. In the interest of maintaining a safe and healthy environment for our students, employees, patients and visitors, the University of Kentucky is a Tobacco & Drug Free campus. The University follows both the federal and state Constitutions as well as all applicable federal and state laws on nondiscrimination. The University provides equal opportunities for qualified persons in all aspects of institutional operations and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ethnic origin, religion, creed, age, physical or mental disability, veteran status, uniformed service, political belief, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, marital status, genetic information or social or economic status or whether the person is a smoker or nonsmoker, as long as the person complies with University regulation concerning smoking. Any candidate offered a position may be required to pass pre-employment screenings as mandated by University of Kentucky Human Resources. These screenings may include a national background check and/or drug screen. Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Specific Request 1 Specific Request 2 Optional Documents 805 Las Cimas Parkway Suite 400 Austin, Texas, 78746 © University of Kentucky Lexington, Kentucky 40506 An Equal Opportunity University Accreditation Directory Email Privacy Policy Copyright © Faber To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://ukjobs.uky.edu/postings/642983": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Social Work",
      "description": "University of Kentucky Applicant Site | Lecturer in Social Work Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Sign Up For Job Alerts! Log In /Create Account Help Applicant Support Lecturer in Social Work Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Posting Details Job Title Lecturer in Social Work Requisition Number FE04967 Working Title Department Name 8T110:College of Social Work Work Location Lexington, KY Type of Position Faculty Position Time Status Full-Time Tenure Track Status Non-Tenure Track Required Education Master of Social Work ( MSW ), Master of Science in Criminal Justice ( MSCJ ), or related field. Required Related Experience Teaching experience at the university level Required License/Registration/Certification N/A Job Summary THE UNIVERSITY OF KENTUCKY , COLLEGE OF SOCIAL WORK (CoSW) is a renowned leader in higher education. Our mission is clear: Through rigorous research, excellence in instruction, and steadfast service, the CoSW works to improve the human condition. Always, in all ways. As the state’s flagship university, CoSW is committed to actualizing our mission through innovative, accessible academic programming. CoSW is home to Bachelor of Arts in Social Work (traditional and online), Bachelor of Arts in Criminal Justice (traditional and online), Bachelor of Science in Criminal Justice (traditional and online), Master of Social Work (hybrid and online), Master of Science in Criminal Justice (online), Doctorate of Social Work (online), PhD in Social Work (traditional), and multiple certificate programs. In addition to Lexington’s main campus, we have a satellite campus at Fort Sam Houston in San Antonio, Texas. To continue to advance our support infrastructure for CoSW, we invite applicants for 12-month, non- tenure eligible Lecturer positions in the Lecturer Title Series to begin as early as Spring or Fall 2027. The University’s mission involves three primary functions across our faculty cohort: instruction, research, and service. Each year, faculty work with the Office of the Dean to delineate their distribution of effort ( DOE ). This DOE outlines how faculty will spend their time. In general, DOE for faculty members in the Lecturer Title Series typically includes allocation in the area of instruction. Of course, the DOE can fluctuate depending on an array of factors (e.g., new course development, service to the college, etc.). While the DOE is reviewed on an ongoing basis, successful candidates are expected to teach undergraduate and graduate courses. It is expected that work in this faculty appointment, including but not limited to course instruction, office hours, administrative meetings, and attending college-wide events such as faculty meetings, will occur on-site. The Lecturer positions will include provision of instruction. Such expectations include conducting classes, seminars, and lectures as part of the courses taught. Additionally, these positions will be utilizing career and working experience to educate students, advise them on both educational and professional levels, and will continuously contribute to their department’s development. While a doctorate in Social Work, Criminal Justice, or a related field is preferred, candidates with an ABD or an appropriate master’s degree and substantial relevant professional experience will be considered. Employment at UK is subject to fund availability and comes with many rewards, focusing on your total well-being and career development, with leadership striving to provide a strong work-life integration. The university generously contributes to employees’ retirement plans, medical coverage, and life insurance. In addition, UK offers optional benefits such as dental and vision insurance, additional retirement plans and much more. To learn more about these benefits, please visit: https://hr.uky.edu/employment/our-benefits . Applicants are requested to submit a curriculum vitae (resume), a letter of interest detailing applicant’s goodness of fit for the position (upload as Cover Letter), contact information for at least three professional references (upload as Specific Request #1), and relevant teaching experience along with evidence of teaching effectiveness for one or more academic years (upload as Specific Request #2). Application reviews will begin right away and continue until the position has been filled. We anticipate this search will run from October through December 2026. Skills / Knowledge / Abilities Does this position have supervisory responsibilities? No Preferred Education/Experience Teaching/instruction in a wide array of courses at multiple levels and formats, including online instruction, is preferred. Deadline to Apply Open Until Filled Yes Our University Community We value the well-being of each of our employees and are dedicated to creating a healthy place for everyone to work, learn and live. In the interest of maintaining a safe and healthy environment for our students, employees, patients and visitors, the University of Kentucky is a Tobacco & Drug Free campus. The University follows both the federal and state Constitutions as well as all applicable federal and state laws on nondiscrimination. The University provides equal opportunities for qualified persons in all aspects of institutional operations and does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, ethnic origin, religion, creed, age, physical or mental disability, veteran status, uniformed service, political belief, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, pregnancy, marital status, genetic information or social or economic status or whether the person is a smoker or nonsmoker, as long as the person complies with University regulation concerning smoking. Any candidate offered a position may be required to pass pre-employment screenings as mandated by University of Kentucky Human Resources. These screenings may include a national background check and/or drug screen. Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Specific Request 1 Specific Request 2 Optional Documents 805 Las Cimas Parkway Suite 400 Austin, Texas, 78746 © University of Kentucky Lexington, Kentucky 40506 An Equal Opportunity University Accreditation Directory Email Privacy Policy Copyright © Faber To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
